<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chapter9" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Determinant</title>

  <introduction>

    <p>
            In this chapter, we will focus on the geometry of the matrix transformations to construct and analyze the concept of the determinant. There are many ways to introduce the determinant and I make the pedagogical decision to develop the determinant through geometry.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert> <xref ref="chp9sec1"/>.</alert> The volume of a region in <m>\R^n</m> is the number of unit cubes needed to cover it. Each matrix transformation <m>T_A</m> scales volumes and this scaling factor <m>\vsf(A)</m> depends only on the matrix and not the region. Of the simple geometric transformations, the only ones with a scaling factor other than 1 are the stretches (and projections). The determinant of a matrix <m>A</m> is the signed volume scaling factor of its associated transformation.
        </p>

    
    <objectives>
            <ul>
            <li>I understand how simple transformations of the plane affect area.</li>
            <li>I understand how to relate how a composition affects area to how each constituent transformation affects area.</li>
            <li>I can generalize my reasoning to volume in <m>\R^n</m>.</li>
            <li>I understand how simple transformations of the plane affect sign.</li>
            <li>I understand how to relate how a composition affects sign to how each constituent transformation affects sign.</li>
            <li>I can generalize my reasoning to sign in <m>\R^n</m>.</li>
            <li>I can reason about the determinant of a matrix by geometrically conceptualizing it as the signed volume scaling factor.</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    
        <p>
            <alert> <xref ref="chp9sec2"/>.</alert> Even though we constructed the determinant through geometric reasoning, algebraic properties emerge. We develop these algebraic properties and relate the determinant to the \TheList.
        </p>

    
    <objectives>
            <ul>
            <li>I can compute the determinant of elementary matrices.</li>
            <li>I understand how to relate the determinant of an arbitrary matrix to the determinants of elementary matrices.</li>
            <li>I understand properties of the determinant both geometrically and algebraically.</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

        <p>
            <alert> <xref ref="chp9sec3"/>.</alert> Having developed geometric meanings and algebraic properties, we conclude this chapter with different strategies for computing the determinant. 
        </p>


    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>I understand that there are many strategies for computing the determinant and can flexible decide which among them to use in a given context.</li>
            <li>I can use <m>PLU</m>-decompositions and cofactor expansion to compute the determinant by hand for small matrices.</li>
            <li>I can use GeoGebra to compute the determinant of matrices.</li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>



  </introduction>

